data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":693,"item_id":"msg_0dcec71cab7a404c016b0320b37f9c87d1ad0e185815d529d2","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\"reviews\":[{\"actor\":\"birch-2\",\"decision\":\"reuse\",\"ability\":\"bark-extraction\",\"reason\":\"The implemented capability already checks actual material suitability and separates usable bark with a real stone or flint, paid labor and finite source depletion. It does not assume that a tree label establishes harvestable trunk bark. The observed object represents two units of branch stock, not represented standing-trunk tissue; gathering exposed stock and peeling owned branches are separate operations. This source alone cannot supply one full bark unit under the existing maximum 10% outer-layer rule: two 1.1-mass branches contain at most 0.22 bark mass, less than the 0.3 mass required for one bark unit. Reuse therefore provides the requested feasible acquisition mechanism, not a promise of sufficient local yield.\",\"specification\":\"Reuse the existing bark-extraction contract unchanged; consult its full schema before individual study and execution. Learning requires two simulation rounds and does not occur during review. Assess the actual source/material and personally inspect the reusable stone contact; ownership of two stones alone does not establish inspected suitability. Existing gather handles only genuinely exposed branch stock within shared interaction geometry, charging its normal costs and decrementing real stock; it does not fell or strip the tree. Peeling costs two active work-round equivalents and two energy per original branch unit, scaled to the actual remaining peelable fraction. Preserve material provenance and conserve bark, core and fines; whole-tenth bark units round down and residual mass remains fines. Never count proposed yield as inventory or as a completed drying tray. Use the existing public parameters, worksite reach/collision checks, escrow ownership, progress and completion rules. Interruption retains actual remaining work and partial materials; death cancels active work and leaves tools and partial material at the worksite without duplication or deletion. No new capability, physical mechanism, dependency, schema migration or duplicate feedback source is needed.\",\"experienceReview\":\"Successful paid peeling can provide limited grounded capability-progress feedback through the existing bark-extraction practice-confirmed rule when its qualification predicate is actually satisfied: performer only, amount 0.4, cap 0.5, hold duration 8 rounds, refresh with 12-round cooldown. Its maintained component gains 0.02 on qualifying events and decays 0.001 per round; mastery or routine unqualified repetition awards nothing. A genuinely new experimentally confirmed fact may separately trigger the existing information-confirmed grounded-understanding rule: qualified performer, amount 0.8, cap 1, duration 8, refresh cooldown 12, maintained gain 0.035 and decay 0.0015. Do not duplicate either functional source under a bark-acquisition alias. Requesting, studying, receiving bark or naming an output grants no happiness; peeling itself provides no established nutrition, healing or physical comfort.\",\"visualReview\":\"Use existing bark-assessed, bark-peeling-started, bark-peeling-progress, bark-peeling-completed and bark-peeling-stopped event bindings. Show actual assessed suitability, paid work progress, exposed core, separated bark/fines, tool location and retained partial work at the shared worksite. Replay must distinguish gathered branch stock from peeling outputs and show real source depletion; never depict trunk stripping, a full bark unit or a completed tray unless actual state supports it. Subscribe to practice-confirmed and information-confirmed only for validated existing experience feedback.\",\"learning\":2}]}","logprobs":[]}

